{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "complaint_categories",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.95,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Directly enables identifying the most common complaint types, the exact grouping axis the query needs. Values are well-grounded: 'plot' for the review criticizing writing/dialogue is defensible, and negative reviews without a specific craft complaint get 'none'. Multi-valued categorization is a sound reading of source text."
    },
    {
      "name": "num_complaints",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A count derivable directly from complaint_categories, so partly redundant with it, but still offers a usable magnitude axis for prioritizing which reviews carry the most fixable issues. Values match the categorization and are well-grounded."
    },
    {
      "name": "sentiment_boost_if_addressed",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.55,
      "source_groundedness": 0.2,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "This column speaks most directly to the query's counterfactual, giving it apparent leverage. However the specific numeric boosts (20, 40, 60) are invented and appear to be a mechanical function of complaint count rather than anything the text supports. The source gives no basis for a quantified sentiment increase, so the values assert unwarranted specifics."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
  "source_groundedness": 0.65,
  "rationale": "complaint_categories is highly useful and well-grounded, giving the core grouping axis the query needs; num_complaints is grounded but partly redundant. sentiment_boost_if_addressed targets the query's counterfactual but its magnitudes are fabricated with no textual basis, dragging table-level groundedness down. Overall the set provides genuine analytical leverage but includes one column that confidently asserts unsupported numeric specifics."
}

